  "text": "We are given a determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix} \n\\cos^2 \\alpha & \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha & \\sin^2 \\alpha \\\\ \n\\cos^2 \\beta & \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta & \\sin^2 \\beta \\\\ \n\\cos^2 \\gamma & \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma & \\sin^2 \\gamma \n\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nand the condition:\n\n$$\n\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma = \\pi\n$$\n\nWe are asked to find the value of the determinant.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understand the structure of the matrix\n\nEach row has:\n\n- First element: $\\cos^2 \\theta$\n- Second element: $\\cos \\theta \\sin \\theta$\n- Third element: $\\sin^2 \\theta$\n\nThis is a **3x3 matrix** with each row containing the same pattern of trigonometric functions.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Use trigonometric identities\n\nNote that:\n\n$$\n\\cos \\theta \\sin \\theta = \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2\\theta\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix becomes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix} \n\\cos^2 \\alpha & \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2\\alpha & \\sin^2 \\alpha \\\\ \n\\cos^2 \\beta & \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2\\beta & \\sin^2 \\beta \\\\ \n\\cos^2 \\gamma & \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2\\gamma & \\sin^2 \\gamma \n\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis is still not immediately helpful.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Use properties of determinants and trigonometric identities\n\nLet’s denote:\n\n$$\nA = \\cos^2 \\alpha, \\quad B = \\cos^2 \\beta, \\quad C = \\cos^2 \\gamma\n$$\n\nThen the matrix becomes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix} \nA & \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2\\alpha & \\sin^2 \\alpha \\\\ \nB & \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2\\beta & \\sin^2 \\beta \\\\ \nC & \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2\\gamma & \\sin^2 \\gamma \n\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the determinant directly.\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Expand the determinant\n\nWe expand along the first row:\n\n$$\n\\text{det} = A \\cdot \\begin{vmatrix} \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2\\beta & \\sin^2 \\beta \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2\\gamma & \\sin^2 \\gamma \\end{vmatrix} \n- \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2\\alpha \\cdot \\begin{vmatrix} \\cos^2 \\beta & \\sin^2 \\beta \\\\ \\cos^2 \\gamma & \\sin^2 \\gamma \\end{vmatrix} \n+ \\sin^2 \\alpha \\cdot \\begin{vmatrix} \\cos^2 \\beta & \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2\\beta \\\\ \\cos^2 \\gamma & \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2\\gamma \\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis looks complicated. But note that the matrix looks symmetric and the entries resemble the identity matrix scaled by trigonometric functions.\n\n---\n\n### Step 5: Use a known identity or symmetry\n\nLet’s consider a general identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 \\theta = \\frac{1 + \\cos 2\\theta}{2}\n$$\n\nSo:\n\n- $\\cos^2 \\alpha = \\frac{1 + \\cos 2\\alpha}{2}$\n- $\\sin^2 \\alpha = \\frac{1 - \\cos 2\\alpha}{2}$\n\nSubstituting into the matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix} \n\\frac{1 + \\cos 2\\alpha}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2\\alpha & \\frac{1 - \\cos 2\\alpha}{2} \\\\ \n\\frac{1 + \\cos 2\\beta}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2\\beta & \\frac{1 - \\cos 2\\beta}{2} \\\\ \n\\frac{1 + \\cos 2\\gamma}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2\\gamma & \\frac{1 - \\cos 2\\gamma}{2} \n\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow we can compute the determinant.\n\n---\n\n### Step 6: Use the identity for the determinant\n\nLet’s denote:\n\n$$\nD = \\begin{vmatrix} \n\\frac{1 + \\cos 2\\alpha}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2\\alpha & \\frac{1 - \\cos 2\\alpha}{2} \\\\ \n\\frac{1 + \\cos 2\\beta}{2} &",
